{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>However, there shall be no needy among you.</b> If you keep sabbatical years and jubilees — for poor people who sell themselves and their daughters return in the sabbatical year, and if he has a debt upon him it is remitted, and in the jubilee his ancestral holdings and his sales return, and it turns out that there shall be no needy among you." "<b>For blessing will bless you.</b> Rather, 'blessing will bless you' refers both to a borrower and to a lender — 'borrower' as explained, and 'lender' even though he remits his loans. <b>For there shall be no needy among you, for blessing will bless you</b> — and you will lose nothing by it. And if you ask: if so, will my loans that the sabbatical year remits be repaid to me? Therefore it says: 'והעבטת גויים רבים' — that you will be able to collect from them. And if you ask: they are not delivered into my hand to come collect — therefore it says: 'ומשלת בגויים רבים' and 'ובך לא ימשולו.'"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:52:53.724112142Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}